Subject: compose: a composed launch presents its own locale, never the operator's shell

Today's packaging fix shipped 440 locale paks where the artifact used to ship
one. That was correct and it turned on a lever that had been dead, and nothing
in the browser was holding the other end. Measured on the new linux-x64
artifact, on a host with `LANG=th_TH.UTF-8` and `LANGUAGE=th`:

    FAIL LOCALE windows  languages=th-TH,th  intl=th
    FAIL LOCALE macos    languages=th-TH,th  intl=th
    FAIL LOCALE linux    languages=th-TH,th  intl=th

A composed Windows persona served Thai language preferences and Thai `Intl`
formatting because the operator's shell said Thai. The same launch also
reported `calendar: "buddhist"`, which no Windows machine in the United States
has ever reported, and which nothing in this repository was looking at.

The launchers were already right. `python/apostate/launch.py` and
`npm/src/index.ts` both write `LANGUAGE`, `LC_ALL`, `LC_MESSAGES` and `LANG` for
every non-host launch, and FINGERPRINTS.md §8 has described that contract since
before the paks shipped. The gap is the bare binary -- `./chrome
--fingerprint-platform=windows`, which is the launch the README leads with.

WHERE THE APPLICATION LOCALE IS RESOLVED, PER PLATFORM

`ResourceBundle::LoadLocaleResources` calls `l10n_util::GetApplicationLocale`,
whose default argument then calls `base::i18n::SetICUDefaultLocale` with the
answer (resource_bundle.cc:507, l10n_util.cc:392-399). That is the one call
that moves `Isolate::DefaultLocale()` and therefore every `Intl` constructor's
default, and the browser reaches it from
`ChromeBrowserMainParts::PreCreateThreadsImpl` through `LoadLocalState` ->
`InitResourceBundleAndDetermineLocale`. What that function resolves *from* is
three different things:

  linux    `GetApplicationLocaleInternalNonMac` takes the
           `defined(USE_GLIB) && !IS_CHROMEOS` branch and reads
           `g_get_language_names()` (l10n_util.cc:344-358), which is glib's
           own LANGUAGE / LC_ALL / LC_MESSAGES / LANG precedence. `pref_locale`
           -- the `--lang` switch and the `kApplicationLocale` pref -- is not
           even pushed as a candidate. The environment is the whole lever, and
           `--lang` moves nothing, which is what the earlier measurement on
           stock Chrome 151 found from the outside.

  macos    `GetApplicationLocaleInternalMac` uses `l10n_util::GetLocaleOverride()`
           first (l10n_util.cc:297-310), which `OverrideLocaleWithCocoaLocale()`
           fills from `base::apple::OuterBundle().preferredLocalizations` in
           `ChromeMainDelegate::PreBrowserMain`. The environment is never
           consulted. Measured on the shipped macos-arm64 bundle:
           `LANG=de_DE.UTF-8 LANGUAGE=de`, `LANGUAGE=th LANG=th_TH.UTF-8` and
           `LC_ALL=fr_FR.UTF-8` each leave `Intl` at en-US.

  windows  `pref_locale` is a candidate first, then `GetLocaleOverrides()`,
           and only if neither answers does it fall through to
           `base::i18n::GetConfiguredLocale()` -- ICU's own default, which
           `uprv_getDefaultLocaleID` takes from `GetUserDefaultLocaleName` on
           that platform rather than from the environment.

So the environment is the Linux lever and only the Linux lever. macOS leaks the
host's *system UI language* by a different route and Windows leaks the host's
*user default locale* by a third; both are pre-existing, neither is what this
patch is about, and both are recorded in `intl.resolved-locale` where the
escalation for them already lives.

WHY THE ENVIRONMENT AND NOT THE RESOLUTION

Two shapes were available. Supplying the composed locale straight to
`GetApplicationLocale` is more precise; writing the environment is what the
launchers do. The second wins on three counts and the third is decisive.

It is one mechanism instead of two. Two mechanisms that disagree are worse than
either, and a launch through the Python package and the same launch by hand have
to present the same locale for the same resolution -- so this reads
`COMPOSED_DEFAULT_LOCALE` as `en-US`, writes the same four variables in the same
forms, and derives the POSIX spelling the same way `_posix_locale` does.

It keeps the C library and ICU agreeing. `intl.resolved-locale` records the
instruction not to force ICU's default while leaving the C library on the
operator's value, because the split is observable through anything that formats
via `strftime` or reads `LANG`. Supplying the locale to `l10n_util` would have
created exactly that split.

And children are covered for free. On Linux the renderer resolves its own
application locale from its own environment through the same glib branch, and
`LaunchProcess` starts from the live `environ` unless a caller overrides it
(launch_posix.cc:308-320), so a browser whose environment is pinned before the
zygote forks hands every descendant the same answer with no second decision to
make. The renderer's `--lang`, which drives `blink::DefaultLanguage()` and with
it locale-dependent font fallback and `<input type=date>`, is also the browser's
resolved locale (render_process_host_impl.cc:3781-3783), so that follows too.

WHERE IT RUNS, AND WHY THAT POINT

`InstallComposedProfile`, which patch 0084 calls from
`ChromeMainDelegate::PreSandboxStartup` in the browser process only. No new call
site and no signature change: the ordering this needs is the ordering 0084
already established.

Against the two hooks this codebase has been bitten at today. Patch 0110 moved
proxy-credential adoption to `BasicStartupComplete` because `Profile::Get()`
caches on first call and that call can precede a later hook; `BasicStartupComplete`
is content_main_runner_impl.cc:903, `InitializeICU` is :965, `PreSandboxStartup`
is :1001. This is later than all three and earlier than everything that matters:
the zygote forks at :1059, where `InitializeZygoteSandboxForBrowserProcess`
"tickles the zygote host so it forks now"; `PreBrowserMain` and its Cocoa
override are later still; and `PreCreateThreadsImpl` -- where the application
locale is actually resolved -- is in `BrowserMainLoop`, long after.
So the compositor has run and a profile exists, and the locale has not been
resolved. The window is wide rather than narrow, which is the property to want.

The read is the one that cannot be raced. The composed locale is taken from
`--fingerprint-locale` off the command line rather than from `Profile::Get()`,
and patch 0111 is why that is complete rather than a shortcut: since 0111 the
switch is the *only* thing that writes `locale.accept_languages` into a composed
profile, so reading the switch and reading the composed profile are the same
answer -- and only one of the two can be pre-cached by whoever calls `Get()`
first. The authored-payload branch, where the switch is inert, reads the payload
the caller is already holding through a new static on `Profile` for the same
reason.

WRITING THE VARIABLES IS NOT ENOUGH

`content/app/content_main.cc:255,263` runs `setlocale(LC_ALL, "")` and
`setlocale(LC_NUMERIC, "C")` before any embedder hook exists, so the C library
has already adopted the operator's environment by the time this runs. glib reads
`LANGUAGE` from the environment live -- so the writes alone do fix the
application locale -- but it falls back to `setlocale(LC_MESSAGES, nullptr)`
when `LANGUAGE` is unset, and every other consumer of the C locale keeps the
operator's value regardless.

Measured, on the host that produced the failure above, with a glib program
running the same calls in the same order:

    host env adopted        LC_MESSAGES=en_US.UTF-8  names[0]=en_US.UTF-8
    composed env written    LC_MESSAGES=en_US.UTF-8  names[0]=de-DE
    + setlocale(LC_ALL,"C") LC_MESSAGES=C            names[0]=de-DE
    + re-adopt              LC_MESSAGES=C            names[0]=de-DE

The second line is the split. So the sequence a launcher-set environment
produces is reproduced exactly: `"C"`, which is the locale a fresh process
starts in, then the two calls content makes. That also reproduces the failure
mode honestly -- when the host has not generated the composed locale, setlocale
refuses it and the C library stays at `"C"` instead of falling back to the
operator's, while `LANGUAGE` still carries the tag and still decides the
application locale. `de_DE.UTF-8` is not generated on that host and the de-DE
launch below works anyway, which is that design measured rather than argued.

`base::Environment::SetVar` and `setlocale` are both documented as unsafe in a
multi-threaded process. `PreSandboxStartup` satisfies that, and content itself
writes `FC_FONTATIONS` from the next statement after it with the comment
"before creating threads".

PRECEDENCE

Unchanged from the launch contract, and now the same three layers in the binary
as in both packages:

  explicit   `--fingerprint-locale=de-DE,de` -> `de-DE`. Also where the
             packages put their prelaunch GeoIP answer, so one switch serves
             an operator's choice and a resolved exit identically.
  composed   no launch layer named one -> `en-US`, the composed default. Not
             nothing: absence of a resolved locale has to mean a defined
             value, or the served locale is a property of the operator's shell.
  host       `--fingerprint=host` and its off/false/0/disable spellings ->
             untouched. Only there is the host the thing being presented.

A child process, and a launch the catalogue cannot serve, are also untouched:
the first inherits a browser that is already pinned, and the second inherits
the host on all twelve axes, so pinning the locale alone would leave one
composed surface on an otherwise entirely host-owned launch.

THE REPORT

`--fingerprint-explain` gains `locale.application`, naming the value and the
layer that owns it, the way the `seed source` line names where a seed came from.
Every defect found in this binary today was invisible partly because the report
did not name the thing that was broken, and this surface was the worked example:
the report printed `locale.accept_languages (inherited)` with the limitation
"the host's own is served unchanged", on a launch that was serving Thai under a
Windows persona.

That line was true only while the artifact shipped one pak and the lever was
dead. It is now false in a second way as well -- an unnamed Accept-Language list
resolves against `IDS_ACCEPT_LANGUAGES` in the *application locale's* bundle, so
with the locale pinned the list a page reads is the composed default's and not
the host's. So the value becomes `(the en-US bundle's default)` on a new
`composed-default` layer, with a note that says where it comes from, and
`host-inherited` is left to `locale.timezone`, which really is the host's
because ICU's zone is a separate producer (patch 0011). The authored-profile
report gains the same line, since the application locale is the one surface an
`--apostate-profile` launch does not leave to the host.

WHAT THE 440 PAKS ALSO TURNED ON

Asked because the paks were dead weight and are now live, and answered by
measurement on the new artifact rather than by reading. Everything below moved
with `LANGUAGE` and could not have moved before, and all of it is readable from
a page:

  `Intl.DateTimeFormat().resolvedOptions().calendar`   `buddhist` under Thai,
  which reformats every year it touches: `1/1/2513`. `gregory` everywhere else
  measured. A hard tell on any persona claiming a Western device.

  `hourCycle`   `h23` under th, ja, de and sv; `h12` under en-US and ar.

  `new Intl.Locale(...).getWeekInfo().firstDay`   7 under en-US/th/ja, 1 under
  de/sv, 6 under ar.

  `new Intl.Collator().compare` order   `['z','ä','a','o']` sorts `aäoz` under
  en-US and `aozä` under sv. Collation is a separate observable from formatting
  and moves independently of the language list.

  Number and date formatting   `(1234567.89).toLocaleString()` is
  `1.234.567,89` under de-DE and `1 234 567,89` under sv (U+00A0). `Intl`
  `DisplayNames`, `RelativeTimeFormat`, `ListFormat` and `timeZoneName: 'long'`
  all localise.

  The shape of `navigator.languages`, not just its contents   `LANGUAGE=th`
  gives `["th-TH","th"]` with no English fallback at all, while ja and ar give
  `["ja","en-US","en"]` and de gives `["de-DE","de","en-US","en"]`. The list is
  a per-pak resource string, so its *length* is a second-order signal.

  `<input type=date>` rendered width   125.33px under en-US, 106.33 under th,
  108.33 under ja, 141.33 under ar, on the same page at the same font size. A
  page reads the host's locale off a UA-rendered form control with no `Intl`
  call at all, so this leak was observable even with `Intl` entirely blocked.

  Locale-dependent font fallback   `measureText` on `漢字直骨相` at 32px gives
  160.0 under en-US and 160.00030517578125 under `LANGUAGE=ja`: a different CJK
  font was selected, because `blink::DefaultLanguage()` follows the renderer's
  `--lang` and that is the browser's application locale.

Two hypotheses were tested and did not hold, recorded so they are not
re-investigated: the UI's RTL flip under `LANGUAGE=ar` did not reach
`getComputedStyle(documentElement).direction`, which stayed `ltr`, and no
scrollbar-side difference was measurable in headless. Every item above is fixed
by the same one lever, because all of them resolve from the application locale.

VERIFICATION

Compiled: `base/apostate/compose.cc`, `profile.cc`, `explain.cc` and
`compose_unittest.cc`, all objects, on the pinned macos-arm64 build.

Run: `base_unittests --gtest_filter='ApostateComposeTest.*'` -- 31 tests pass,
including the five this adds and the two existing assertions it changes.

Measured on the shipped linux-x64 artifact, on the Thai host. The patch's whole
effect on that platform is that the browser's own four variables are the
composed launch's, so the four arms were measured by giving the unmodified
binary exactly the environment the patch installs -- the glib measurement above
is what makes the two states identical -- with the operator's shell left at
`LANG=th_TH.UTF-8 LANGUAGE=th` throughout:

    pre-fix,  composed windows persona   intl=th     languages=th-TH,th  cal=buddhist
    post-fix, same launch                intl=en-US  languages=en-US,en  cal=gregory
    post-fix, --fingerprint-locale=de-DE,de
                                         intl=de     languages=de-DE,de  1.234.567,89
    --fingerprint=host, untouched        intl=th     languages=th-TH,th  cal=buddhist

And `scripts/checks/release-smoke.mjs` itself, unmodified, against the same
binary with the same substitution: all twelve checks pass, where three failed
before. The command that confirms it on the next build, and its expected
output, are in docs/FINGERPRINTS.md §8.

Not verified, and stated rather than implied: this has not run inside a linked
browser, because the next build has not happened. What the substitution does not
cover is the two lines of C++ that place the write -- that
`InstallComposedProfile` is reached in the browser at that point and that
`Environment::SetVar` lands before the resolution -- and those are the lines
the post-build run of the check above exists to confirm.

Ledger: intl.resolved-locale, navigator.languages, http.accept-language,
coh.intl-locale-vs-navigator-language, coh.locale-language-agreement.

